# Arrivals board layout

Domain boards under `registry/tfl/arrivals/rail-arrivals-board.tsx` and `bus-arrivals-board.tsx`. Shared presentation lives in `arrivals-board-view.tsx`. Rail and bus do **not** share a high-level `variant` API.

## Rhythm

- One unit: `--arrivals-unit` (`0.5rem`). One row: `--arrivals-row` = 6 units (`3rem` / 48px).
- Stop title, line headers, bound labels, and arrival rows are each **exactly one tile** (`TILE_CLASS`: fixed height + `box-border` + `overflow-clip`). `overflow-clip` locks the box without creating a scroll container — do not put `overflow-hidden` on destination cells or row tiles; the page track (`overflow-x-auto`) is the only horizontal scroller.
- Do **not** reserve a double-height title band “in case it wraps”. That leaves a dead gap under short names and desyncs rail vs bus.
- **No gap** between the stop title and the first line group, or between line sections — last arrival row of one group sits flush against the next line header (continuous tile stack). Do not use `space-y-2` / `mt-2` on the board root.

## Borders and bars (never grow a tile)

- **Row separators** — absolute pseudo-elements (`after:` at the bottom). Never `border-b` on arrival/bound rows if that would stack outside the box without `box-border` discipline; prefer absolute hairlines.
- **Line / route brand bar** — solid colours: `border-b-4` + `box-border` so the bar is **inside** the tile height. Do **not** put an in-flow `h-1` div + `mt-*` under the name (that shifts baselines between rail and bus).
- **Striped** Overground / Elizabeth bars — absolute `LineColorBar` pinned to the tile bottom (border cannot paint dual rails).
- **Shared-track merged headers** (`lineGroups`) — `LineName` in `text-foreground` (not a single line colour) plus an absolute segmented bar (one flex stripe per member), matching the Line title “Shared-track group” demo. Not `LineBadgeGroup` (that is the chip).
- Content that does not fit clips; it must not expand the tile (`min/max-height` locked to `--arrivals-row`).

## Side-by-side alignment

When two boards share a homepage/grid row, their stop titles and first content rows must land on the same horizontal lines. Same title height, no title→body gap, same row unit.

## Responsive layout API

Boards are single-column by default; consumers arrange groups/bounds via `className` (root) + `classNames` (`groups`, `group`, `subgroups`, `subgroup`, `rows`). Rules:

- **CSS only.** Container queries, grid/flex, data-slot selectors, custom properties. Never measure widths in JS or add resize-driven state for layout.
- **Containers:** board root = `@container/arrivals`; every line/route section = `@container/arrivals-group`. Bound-column classes on `subgroups` must query `/arrivals-group` (the line's own width), never `/arrivals`.
- **No new wrappers.** `groups` styles the existing sections wrapper, `subgroups` the bounds `<ul>`, `subgroup` the bound `<li>`, `rows` the rows `<ul>`. Slots live on real semantic elements (`data-slot="arrivals-board|arrivals-groups|arrivals-group|arrivals-subgroups|arrivals-subgroup|arrivals-rows|arrivals-row"`).
- **Defaults:** groups/subgroups containers are `grid grid-cols-1` so consumer `grid-cols-*` variants merge without fighting a base `flex`. Sections/subgroups carry `min-w-0` so grid tracks can't be blown out by content.
- **Rhythm holds in columns.** Tiles stay fixed-height; sections of different heights sit side by side — do not pad groups to equal height. No vertical gaps appear between stacked sections (`gap-x` only).
- Domain attributes stay useful: `data-line` on rail sections + headers, `data-bound` on bound subgroups, `data-route` on bus route sections.

## Stop / station title

- Render the human stop name only. **Do not** put NaPTAN / stop-point ids in the UI (dev meta — keep as deprecated unused prop if needed for call-site compat).
- Bus stop letter (e.g. `G`) may sit inline as a red circle; it is a stop property, not per-row.
- Fit with `StationName` (`layout="auto"`, `maxLines={1}`, `allowAbbreviation`, `allowScaleDown`). Prefer full name → abbr → scale. No CSS truncate that hides the name; no layout growth from wrapping.
- Screen readers / copy keep the full canonical name (`StationName` contract).

## Destinations

- Same `StationName` fit policy. Rows stay fixed `--arrivals-row` height; wrap only inside that box (overflow clipped), never grow the row.
- When nothing fits, `formatStationLabel` must keep the **smallest** attempt — never fall back to full-size overflow (see `lib/tfl/station-typography.ts`).

## Rail vs bus rows

- Rail: platform chip only when platforms **vary** inside the bound (compact `P{n}`). When every row shares one platform, hoist it into the bound heading (`Eastbound · Platform 1` or `Platform A`) and omit the row chip. Mixed-line (`lineGroups`) sections add a line chip before the destination. One identity → `LineBadge` (3-letter). A train listed on 2–3 lines → `LineBadgeGroup variant="codes"` (same-height stripe stack; CSS-cycles one 3-letter abbr). Both line chips use a fixed `w-[5ch]` box (`ARRIVALS_IDENTITY_CHIP_WIDTH_CLASS`), same as `BusNumberChip`, so CIR / H&C / MET and the stacked codes chip align. The codes chip must fit inside `--arrivals-row` (48px) at normal chip height (`h-5`).
- Bus: `BusNumberChip` per row (bus red); stop letter on the header only.
- Both chips: keep normal casing (`Platform 4`, route codes as given). Center with `[text-box:trim-both_cap_alphabetic]` (`CHIP_CAP_TEXT_BOX_CLASS`) — uppercase/cap metrics for the box, not `ex` trim. Do not force `uppercase` paint.
- Bound labels (Northbound…) match line-name size; same hairline weight as arrival rows inside a line group.

## Pagination

Shared in `arrivals-bound-group.tsx` (`BoundPager` + `ArrivalsPageTrack`) for rail bounds, bus route groups, and flat bus lists.

- Pages are real horizontal slides (`overflow-x-auto` + `snap-x snap-mandatory` + `snap-always`). Swipe is native OS scrolling — not a JS gesture library.
- Each slide is `w-full min-w-full shrink-0` (and the track is `min-w-0`). `min-w-full` alone with `width: auto` sizes the slide to the destination’s max-content; `StationName` then measures a box that already “fits” and never wraps or abbreviates. Destination fit is board-owned — paged and unpaged rows share one path. Demos pass `pageSize` / `groupBy` / `classNames`; they do not restyle the track.
- Mid-swipe: `gap-x-6` between slides (same as bound-column arrangements) so countdown never touches the next page’s chips; when snapped, each slide is full-width so the gap sits off-screen.
- For rail, a configured positive `pageSize` is a fixed subgroup height, not only a chunk size. Every bound occupies exactly `pageSize` arrival-row tiles, including a short single page and an empty seeded group. For grouped and flat bus boards, apply the same fixed height once `pageCount > 1`; an unpaginated short bus list keeps its natural height. `pageSize <= 0` always keeps the natural unpaged height.
- On a short final page with at least one arrival, use the final spare tile for **No more arrivals**. Any spare tiles between the last arrival and that message show a quiet dash. Do not add a page only to show the message, and do not displace an arrival when `pageSize` leaves no spare tile.
- The end message has a CSS-first width ladder: **No more arrivals** → **No more**. Keep the full phrase as its accessible name. Do not wrap, clip, ellipsise, or reduce the tile height to make it fit.
- Zero arrivals are not an end-of-list state. Keep **No information** in the first tile and use quiet dashes in the remaining tiles. Keep decorative dash tiles out of the accessibility tree.
- Pagination controls follow their tile ownership. When arrows and the `n/m` count share a marked tile with other useful content, such as a rail bound label or grouped bus route header, hide them on hover-capable pointers until group hover or a `:focus-visible` descendant (keyboard). Do not use `:focus-within` — a mouse click would leave the overlay stuck after unhover. When the controls own a dedicated otherwise-empty tile, as on the flat bus list, they are the tile marker and remain visible on hover-capable pointers. Never leave a blank control tile waiting for hover.
- Touch / no-hover controls are always visible because there is no hover affordance. Keep the half-opacity iOS-style dots in the corresponding control position and swipe the rows track to change page.
- Arrow clicks jump instantly (`scrollIntoView` with `behavior: "auto"`). Keyboard focus must reveal any hover-hidden controls.
- Hide the entire pager when `pageCount <= 1` (no lone `1/1` or single dot).

For the default `pageSize={3}`, the final page resolves as follows:

| Arrivals on final page | Tile 1 | Tile 2 | Tile 3 |
|---|---|---|---|
| 0 | No information | dash | dash |
| 1 | arrival | dash | No more arrivals |
| 2 | arrival | arrival | No more arrivals |
| 3 | arrival | arrival | arrival |

For rail, the same rule applies when the final page is the only page. For bus, it applies only when pagination is active. Exact multiples of `pageSize` stay full and do not gain a terminal page. With `pageSize={1}`, show the arrival or empty state; there is no spare tile for an end message.

## Destination filtering

- Do not add a destination-filter prop. Filtering belongs in the consumer before it passes `data` to the board.
- Add a rail documentation example that filters `RealtimePrediction[]` to a named terminus, then renders the filtered array. Prefer the canonical destination field over `towards`, which may be blank or generic.
- Be precise about what the example proves: a train's destination identifies its terminus. It does not prove that the train calls at an arbitrary intermediate station; that requires route or calling-pattern data.
- Give a zero-match filter an accurate, destination-specific empty message. Do not let a consumer-created empty result read as an API failure. Watch line seeding: passing `lines` can create per-line empty groups and bypass the board-level `emptyMessage`.

## Anti-patterns

- Double-height title reservation that sits empty when the name is one line
- Showing stop-point ids next to the title
- `space-y-2` / `mt-2` / `space-y-8` between title and first line group
- Truncating stop names with ellipsis instead of abbr/scale
- In-flow brand bar (`h-1` / `mt-*` under the line name) that shifts the painted baseline
- Flow `border-b` on arrival rows **without** keeping the bar inside a `box-border` fixed tile (prefer absolute hairlines)
- Letting wrap, padding, or borders increase row height and break the baseline grid
- Blank padding tiles on a paged board; use decorative dashes and the final end-of-list message above
- A dedicated pagination tile whose controls remain invisible until hover
- Claiming **No more trains**, **No trains available**, or **End of schedule** when the board only knows the predictions it was given
- Adding a destination-filter prop to the board
- Wrapping homepage proof boards in overlay links to docs — use a hero CTA (e.g. Browse components) instead
